---
import AlternativesListLayout from '../../../layouts/AlternativesListLayout.astro';
import { SUNO_ALTERNATIVES, CRITERIA } from '../../../data/alternatives';

/**
 * Rewritten from the two-column AlternativeLayout to a ranked list, because the
 * top 10 for "suno alternative" is entirely listicles, down to DR 6 sites.
 * The old shape could not satisfy that intent no matter how good the copy was.
 * Verified 14 September 2026: 350/mo US, 2,500 global, KD 0, $1.30 CPC.
 */

const quickAnswer = `
  <p><strong>if you want editable parts rather than a finished mix, Sonura is the pick here:</strong>
  stems on every plan including free, and an AU and VST3 plugin so the parts land in the DAW you
  already use. if you want a finished song with vocals and written lyrics in one pass, Suno is still
  the strongest thing on this page and nothing below changes that.</p>
  <p>the seven below are ranked on what you can actually leave with. every spec is dated, every
  competitive claim links its source, and every entry says who should skip it, ours included.</p>
`;

const stillRightBody = `
  <p>if the job is a finished song, with vocals and written lyrics, in one pass, Suno is still the
  strongest option on this page and the honest recommendation. v5.5 exports wav, per-clip wav, and
  midi out of Suno Studio, and it passed two million paid subscribers and $300m in annual recurring
  revenue in February 2026, so it is not a product that is going anywhere. up to 12 stems are
  available, though only inside Suno Studio on the top plan.</p>
  <p>what Suno does not hand you is a settled answer on training data. there is a licensing deal with
  Warner Music, and litigation with labels it has not signed is still unresolved. that is not a reason
  to avoid it if what you want is a song. it is a reason to know what you are getting before you
  build a release around it.</p>
  <p>plenty of producers use both, and that is a reasonable answer rather than a diplomatic one:
  sketch the idea as a full song in Suno, then build the releasable version out of parts you can
  open up. they are doing different jobs.</p>
`;

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'What is the best Suno alternative?',
    answer:
      'It depends on what you need to leave with, which is why the list above is ranked that way. For editable parts and stems you finish in a DAW, Sonura. For the deepest post-generation toolkit on a song platform, Mureka, which does 5-stem and 12-stem separation. For the cleanest rights story on a model you can actually export from, ElevenLabs Music v2. For running a song model on your own hardware, MiniMax Music 3.0.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is there a free Suno alternative?',
    answer:
      'Yes. Sonura is free to start with no credit card, and free output is released under CC0, so nothing blocks you commercially, though it is public domain rather than exclusively yours. MiniMax Music 3.0 publishes its weights on Hugging Face, so it is free to run if you have the GPU for it. Paid Sonura plans keep your work private and exclusively yours with no royalty splits.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which Suno alternative gives you stems?',
    answer:
      'Three of the seven. Sonura splits any track into stems on every plan including free. Mureka O2 does 5-stem and 12-stem separation. Udio supports stems in principle but has download switched off during the UMG transition, so you cannot currently retrieve them. ElevenLabs, Lyria, MiniMax, and Stable Audio 3 all return a finished mix instead.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why can I not download from Udio anymore?',
    answer:
      'Udio settled with Universal Music Group and became a closed platform during the transition. Audio, stem, and video download are all disabled, per <a href="https://help.udio.com/en/articles/12683565-changes-associated-with-the-universal-music-group-umg-partnership" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-link">Udio\'s own help centre</a>. Its licensing is the best in the category, which is exactly what makes the situation frustrating rather than simple.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which AI music tool has the cleanest training data?',
    answer:
      'Stable Audio 3 is trained on fully licensed data plus creative commons material, which is the clearest position of anything here. Udio is licensed through Universal, Warner, Merlin, and Kobalt, opt-in for the artists and songwriters at each, which is the best story among the song platforms. ElevenLabs is licensed through Merlin and Kobalt on a 50/50 royalty split. Mureka, MiniMax, and Lyria have not disclosed in detail.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I use a Suno alternative commercially?',
    answer:
      'Check each one, because the answer genuinely differs. Sonura gives exclusive rights on every paid plan with no royalty splits. ElevenLabs allows it but its self-serve plans carve out film, tv, and games. MiniMax ships a custom community licence you should read against your use. Stable Audio 3 offers an enterprise licence with legal indemnification above $1m revenue. Lyria is covered by Google Cloud terms rather than a music licence.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Do I have to switch away from Suno completely?',
    answer:
      'No, and most people should not. Suno is the strongest option here for a finished song with vocals in one pass. The common pattern is to sketch in Suno and build the releasable version out of editable parts, which is the job Sonura is built for. Using both is a legitimate answer, not a hedge.',
  },
];

const sources = [
  {
    label: 'Udio Help Center — changes associated with the Universal Music Group partnership (audio, video and stem downloads disabled)',
    url: 'https://help.udio.com/en/articles/12683565-changes-associated-with-the-universal-music-group-umg-partnership',
  },
  {
    label: 'Music Business Worldwide — Udio licensing deals with Universal, Warner, Merlin and Kobalt',
    url: 'https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/after-universal-warner-and-merlin-deals-now-udio-inks-licensing-agreement-with-kobalt/',
  },
  {
    label: 'Suno — v5.5 release notes, 26 March 2026',
    url: 'https://suno.com/blog/v5-5',
  },
  {
    label: 'TechCrunch — Suno hits 2 million paid subscribers and $300M ARR, February 2026',
    url: 'https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/27/ai-music-generator-suno-hits-2-million-paid-subscribers-and-300m-in-annual-recurring-revenue/',
  },
  {
    label: 'Music Business Worldwide — UMG and Sony still litigating against Suno',
    url: 'https://www.musicbusinessworldwide.com/suno-fights-to-keep-warner-music-settlement-terms-away-from-umg-and-sony/',
  },
  {
    label: 'Warner Music Group and Suno partnership announcement, November 2025',
    url: 'https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/warner-music-group-and-suno-forge-groundbreaking-partnership-302626017.html',
  },
];
---

<AlternativesListLayout
  competitor="Suno"
  competitorSlug="suno-alternative"
  title="7 Best Suno Alternatives in 2026, Ranked and Dated | Sonura"
  description="The best Suno alternatives, ranked on what you can actually export. Stems, licensing, and commercial rights compared across seven tools, with sources and dates."
  keywords="suno alternative, suno ai alternative, best suno alternatives, better than suno, suno competitor, free suno alternative, suno alternative with stems, ai music generator alternative"
  headline="Best Suno Alternatives in 2026"
  quickAnswer={quickAnswer}
  entries={SUNO_ALTERNATIVES}
  criteria={CRITERIA}
  stillRightTitle="When Suno is still the right call"
  stillRightBody={stillRightBody}
  faqs={faqs}
  sources={sources}
  finalCtaTitle="Parts you can open up"
  finalCtaSubtitle="Beats, loops, vocals, and stems that drop into your DAW, with stem export on every plan. Free to start, no credit card."
  finalCtaButtonText="Start free"
/>
